  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\nThe marine layer was around 3500 ft deep this morning, with low \nclouds filling in the coastal basin. Visible satellite from this \nmorning even showed low clouds through the Cajon Pass. At 11 AM, \nsatellite was still showing patchy low clouds along the coast and \nportions of the western valleys. The marine layer is expected to \nstay a similar depth tonight, with similar inland extent expected. \nThe marine layer will become shallower for Friday into the weekend \nwith night and morning coastal low clouds not spreading inland past \nthe far western valleys.\n\nWeak troughing will gradually move east into the Great Basin by this \nweekend. That will result in below cooler conditions through \nThursday, with a gradual warming Friday through Sunday as weak \ntransient ridging builds over the West Coast. The warming will be\nmost noticeable for inland locations. Even with the warming \ndaytime highs, overnight lows will continue to cool off helping \novernight relief from the heat. \n\nAnother trough of low pressure is expected to move west across \nSouthern California Monday which will bring a few degrees of cooling \nand deepen the marine layer again. By the middle of next week, there \nstarts to be some spread in the upper level pattern. NBM guidance\nhas an 8 degree difference between the 75th (warmer end of \nsolutions) and 25th (cooler end of solutions) percentile \ntemperatures for the inland valleys. The current forecast follows \nthe NBM which has temperatures within a few degrees of average for\nmost locations.",
